# [Maximum Length of Pair Chain](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-length-of-pair-chain/)

## Approach: Recursion + Memoization (1D/2D DP)

- Sort the pairs in ascending lexicographical order.
- At each index, choose whether to include the current pair or skip it.
- Include the pair only when `pairs[curr][0] > pairs[prev][1]`, ensuring the chain condition is satisfied.
- Use memoization to store the maximum chain length for each `(curr, prev)` state.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int dp[1001][1001];

    int Longest_chain(vector<vector<int>>& pairs, int curr, int prev) {
        if (curr >= pairs.size()) return 0;

        if (dp[curr][prev + 1] != -1)
            return dp[curr][prev + 1];

        int take = 0;

        if (prev == -1 || pairs[curr][0] > pairs[prev][1]) {
            take = 1 + Longest_chain(pairs, curr + 1, curr);
        }

        int not_take = Longest_chain(pairs, curr + 1, prev);

        return dp[curr][prev + 1] = max(take, not_take);
    }

    int findLongestChain(vector<vector<int>>& pairs) {
        memset(dp, -1, sizeof(dp));
        sort(pairs.begin(), pairs.end());

        return Longest_chain(pairs, 0, -1);
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n^2)\), since there are \(O(n^2)\) memoization states, each taking constant time apart from recursive calls.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(n^2)\) for the DP table, plus \(O(n)\) recursion stack space.

Here, \(n\) is the number of pairs.

